---
/**
 * /calculator/one-rep-max — 1 Rep Max Calculator Page
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Target keyword:    "1 rep max calculator"
 * Secondary:         "one rep max calculator", "1rm calculator bench press", "epley formula calculator"
 * Layout:            CalculatorLayout
 * Widget:            OneRepMaxCalculator.astro
 */
import CalculatorLayout from '../../layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import OneRepMaxCalculator from '../../components/calculators/OneRepMaxCalculator.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How accurate is a 1RM calculator?',
    answer: 'For reps between 2 and 10, 1RM calculators are highly accurate, typically estimating your biological maximum to within ±3% to 5% of actual test values. However, as the reps performed exceed 10, the accuracy drops significantly because muscular endurance and cardiovascular stamina become limiting factors instead of absolute skeletal muscle force.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the Epley formula?',
    answer: 'The Epley formula is the most widely recognized clinical regression equation for predicting maximum strength. Created by Allan Epley in 1985, it calculates 1RM using the equation: 1RM = Weight × (1 + Reps / 30). It is considered highly accurate for compound movements like the bench press, squat, and deadlift.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I test my 1RM directly or use a calculator?',
    answer: 'For beginners, intermediates, and older adults, using a calculator is highly recommended over direct testing. Direct 1RM testing places extreme mechanical stress on tendons, ligaments, and joints, which can cause severe injury if proper lifting form breaks down. Direct testing is typically reserved for experienced competitive athletes (like powerlifters or weightlifters) who have mastered lifting mechanics under maximal loads.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How often should I recalculate my 1RM?',
    answer: 'You should recalculate your 1RM every 4 to 8 weeks, depending on your training block. If you are consistently applying progressive overload (increasing reps or weight over time), your estimated 1RM will increase. Periodically updating your 1RM ensures your training intensity percentages remain aligned with your current strength capacity.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What weight percentage of 1RM should I use for hypertrophy?',
    answer: 'For hypertrophy (muscle growth), the scientific consensus suggests training in the range of 70% to 80% of your 1RM, which typically corresponds to performing sets of 6 to 12 repetitions. This load range maximizes mechanical tension and metabolic stress — the two primary drivers of muscle growth.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I use a 1RM calculator for bodyweight exercises?',
    answer: 'Yes, but you must factor in your total body weight. For bodyweight exercises like pull-ups or dips, add your current body weight to the added external weight (if any) before running the calculation. For example, if you weigh 80 kg and perform 5 pull-ups with a 20 kg weighted vest, use 100 kg as the "Weight Lifted" in the calculator, then subtract your 80 kg body weight from the final 1RM result.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a good 1RM for bench press / squat / deadlift?',
    answer: 'Strength levels vary by gender, body weight, and training age. A common biological strength standard for healthy, drug-free adult males is a 1.0× bodyweight bench press, 1.5× bodyweight squat, and 1.75× bodyweight deadlift. For females, the standard is 0.75× bodyweight bench press, 1.0× bodyweight squat, and 1.25× bodyweight deadlift. Exceeding these standards places you in the advanced category.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does 1RM change if I lose or gain weight?',
    answer: 'Yes. Losing weight (particularly during a caloric deficit) can sometimes cause a slight drop in absolute 1RM due to changes in leverage, glycogen levels, and potential loss of lean muscle mass. Conversely, a caloric surplus typically raises your 1RM by improving recovery and increasing muscle cross-sectional area.',
  },
];

const relatedCalculators = [
  {
    name: 'TDEE Calculator',
    href: '/calculator/tdee',
    emoji: '⚡',
    description: 'Find your total daily calorie burn',
  },
  {
    name: 'Protein Intake',
    href: '/calculator/protein',
    emoji: '🥩',
    description: 'Calculate daily protein requirements',
  },
  {
    name: 'Lean Body Mass',
    href: '/calculator/lean-mass',
    emoji: '💪',
    description: 'Find your skeletal muscle weight',
  },
  {
    name: 'Calorie Target',
    href: '/calculator/calories',
    emoji: '🍎',
    description: 'Find daily calorie needs for your goal',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  title="1 Rep Max Calculator — Epley, Brzycki & More"
  description="Calculate your one rep max (1RM) using 4 proven formulas including Epley and Brzycki. Get instant training percentages for strength, hypertrophy and endurance. Free, private, no sign-up."
  keyword="1 rep max calculator"
  canonicalPath="/calculator/one-rep-max"
  formula="Epley, Brzycki, Lander & Lombardi Equations"
  lastModified={new Date('2026-07-12')}
  faqs={faqs}
  relatedCalculators={relatedCalculators}
  accuracyRating="±3–5%"
>

  <!-- slot: calculator -->
  <OneRepMaxCalculator slot="calculator" />

  <!-- slot: what-is -->
  <Fragment slot="what-is">
    <p>
      Your <strong>one rep max (1RM)</strong> is the maximum amount of weight you can lift for a single
      repetition of a given exercise with proper form. It represents the absolute limit of your
      musculoskeletal system's contractile force. Rather than trying to lift a dangerous weight directly,
      a 1RM calculator allows you to safely estimate this value based on lighter weights you can lift for
      multiple repetitions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Understanding your 1RM is essential for structured strength programming and applying <strong>progressive
      overload</strong>. Modern sports science programs prescribe exercise intensity as a percentage of your
      1RM (e.g., performing squats at 80% of your 1RM). Without knowing this number, you are forced to guess
      your training weights, which often leads to under-training (failing to stimulate muscle growth) or
      over-training (causing excessive fatigue or injury).
    </p>
    <p>
      1RM tracking is utilized across all fitness disciplines. Powerlifters and weightlifters track 1RM to
      monitor competition progress, while bodybuilders use 1RM percentages to program hypertrophy zones
      and ensure they are lifting with sufficient mechanical tension. Even general fitness programs rely on
      estimated 1RM scores to monitor recovery, adjust training volumes, and ensure consistent physical progress
      over time.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- slot: how-to -->
  <Fragment slot="how-to">
    <p>
      Testing your maximum capacity directly is highly taxing on the central nervous system and carries
      a high risk of acute joint and tendon injuries. Multi-rep estimation formulas allow you to find your
      limits safely.
    </p>

    <div class="formula-block" role="math" aria-label="Epley 1RM formula">
      <p class="formula-label">The Epley Formula (Standard)</p>
      <code class="formula-code">1RM = Weight Lifted × (1 + Reps ÷ 30)

Worked Example:
   If you lift 100 kg for 5 reps:
   1RM = 100 × (1 + 5 ÷ 30)
   1RM = 100 × (1 + 0.1667)
   1RM = 100 × 1.1667 = 116.7 kg</code>
      <p class="formula-source">
        Source: Epley, A. (1985). "Poundage Chart." <em>Boyd Epley Workout</em>. Lincoln, NE.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>The Four Clinical Formulas</h3>
    <p>
      Different math equations model muscular fatigue differently. Our calculator includes the four most
      validated formulas in sports science:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Epley:</strong> The industry standard, highly reliable for general lifting and compound exercises.</li>
      <li><strong>Brzycki:</strong> Created by Roman Brzycki, this equation assumes a linear fatigue curve and is excellent for lower rep counts (2 to 8 reps).</li>
      <li><strong>Lander:</strong> Establishes progressive fatigue coefficients and scales effectively for leg presses and heavy squatting.</li>
      <li><strong>Lombardi:</strong> A power-based regression formula developed by researcher Lombardi, useful for quick estimations.</li>
    </ul>

    <blockquote style="margin: 24px 0; padding-left: 16px; border-left: 4px solid var(--color-primary); font-style: italic;">
      <strong>Note:</strong> All mathematical formulas become progressively less accurate as repetitions exceed 10.
      For the most accurate estimation, perform your multi-rep sets using a weight that limits you to between 2 and 6 repetitions.
    </blockquote>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- slot: ranges -->
  <Fragment slot="ranges">
    <p>
      Once your 1RM is calculated, you can divide your training into specific intensity percentages to target
      different physical adaptations:
    </p>

    <h3>1RM Intensity Percentage Zones</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>1RM % Range</th>
          <th>Rep Range</th>
          <th>Primary Training Effect</th>
          <th>Recommended Rest</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>85% to 95%</strong></td>
          <td>1 to 5 reps</td>
          <td>Maximal Strength & Central Nervous System Recruitment</td>
          <td>3 to 5 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>70% to 80%</strong></td>
          <td>6 to 12 reps</td>
          <td>Myofibrillar Hypertrophy (Muscle Growth) & Mechanical Tension</td>
          <td>90 to 120 seconds</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>60% to 70%</strong></td>
          <td>12 to 15 reps</td>
          <td>Muscular Endurance & Metabolic Stress</td>
          <td>60 to 90 seconds</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Below 60%</strong></td>
          <td>15+ reps</td>
          <td>Aerobic Capacity & Capillary Density</td>
          <td>30 to 60 seconds</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Practical Application Example</h3>
    <p>
      If your bench press 1RM is estimated at **100 kg** (or lbs):
      To build strength, your primary working sets should target **85 kg to 90 kg** for sets of 3 to 5 reps.
      If your goal for the next block is hypertrophy, you should perform your working sets at **70 kg to 80 kg**
      for sets of 8 to 10 reps.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

</CalculatorLayout>

<style>
  /* Local styling overrides if needed, sharing formula block design */
  .formula-block {
    margin: 1.5em 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    border-radius: 4px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .formula-label {
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--color-mute);
    padding: 10px 16px 6px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .formula-code {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    padding: 16px;
    white-space: pre;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .formula-source {
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    color: var(--color-mute);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    margin: 0;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>
